From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jayden Milne <gnss-share@jayd.ml>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 16:11:14 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 4/4] gnss: add a qmi_loc driver for Qualcomm modems

Reads NMEA from the GNSS engine of a Qualcomm modem DSP through its QMI
location service (LOC, QRTR service 0x10 version 2). This is for modems
that ModemManager does not manage, e.g. the GNSS-only modem images of APQ
SoCs, which offer LOC but no WDS, DMS or NAS, so the mm driver can't be
used.

The session starts when the first client connects and stops when the last
one leaves: REG_EVENTS (NMEA, time requests, engine and session state),
SET_OPERATION_MODE standalone (there is no A-GPS server), START (session
1, periodic, intermediate reports, 1000 ms) and STOP. Closing the socket,
also when gnss-share dies, makes the kernel send DEL_CLIENT to the modem.
The service is found through the QRTR name service, not at a fixed port,
and the lookup stays open: when the modem restarts, the session is set up
again on the new service. A request that fails or isn't answered is
retried after 30 s.

The system time is injected (INJECT_UTC_TIME) after START and when the
engine asks for it, only while the kernel's maximum error estimate for
the clock is below NTP_PHASE_LIMIT (16 s), the test systemd uses for "NTP
synchronized"; that estimate, at least 500 ms, is the uncertainty sent.

Message IDs, TLVs and enum values are from libqmi's
data/qmi-service-loc.json, qmi-enums-loc.h and qmi-flags64-loc.h; the
sequence is close to ModemManager's (src/mm-shared-qmi.c). NMEA text is
never logged. Load and Save do nothing: the modem keeps its own
assistance data.

Tested on x86_64, aarch64 (qemu-user), armv7 (qemu-user) and x86: unit
tests against a fake socket (setup, NMEA, modem restart, unsynchronized
clock, failures and timeouts, socket errors) and a test over the kernel's
QRTR with a fake service, which skips without AF_QIPCRTR.
